          Concrete leveling typically costs roughly $3 to $6 per square foot with traditional mudjacking and roughly $5 to $25 per square foot with polyurethane foam, which works out to about $500 to $2,500 for most residential jobs such as a sunken sidewalk section, a few driveway panels or a settled patio. That is usually 50 to 75 percent less than tearing out and repouring the same concrete. The price swings with how deep the void is, how many slabs need lifting, the contractor's job minimum and your region, and prices change year to year, so treat every figure here as a typical US range rather than a quote. The real decision is not just which method is cheaper, but whether the slab is worth lifting at all.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does Concrete Leveling Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical homeowner, the bill for concrete leveling lands somewhere between about $500 and $2,500. A single settled sidewalk square or a front stoop that has tipped toward the house often hits the bottom of that range, mostly because of the contractor's minimum charge. Several sunken driveway panels, a garage apron and a patio done in one visit can push past $2,500, especially with foam. Very large jobs, such as an entire pool deck or a long run of driveway, can reach $4,000 or more, though the per-square-foot rate usually drops as the job grows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per square foot, mudjacking (also called slabjacking or concrete raising) is the cheaper method, commonly around $3 to $6 per square foot. Polyurethane foam leveling, sometimes sold as polyjacking or foam jacking, typically runs around $5 to $25 per square foot depending on how much foam the void swallows. The wide foam range is not padding: foam is priced by the pound of material injected, and a slab with a shallow gap needs far less than one sitting over a washed-out hole.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$3-6</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mudjacking per sq ft, typical</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$5-25</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Foam per sq ft, typical</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$300-800</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common job minimum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50-75%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usual savings vs replacing</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Compare that with replacement. Tearing out and repouring a plain concrete slab commonly costs around $8 to $18 per square foot once demolition, hauling, base repair, forms and new concrete are included. On a 400 square foot section of driveway, leveling might cost $1,500 to $3,000 while replacement might cost $4,000 to $7,000. That gap is why leveling is the default recommendation for a slab that is sound but sunken, and why it is worth getting at least one leveling quote before you agree to a full tear-out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that leveling only fixes height. It does not fix cracks, spalling, scaling or a slab that has broken into many pieces. If the concrete surface is in poor shape, you will be paying to lift something that still looks bad and may keep deteriorating. The rest of this guide covers how to tell which situation you are in, what pushes the price up, and how to make sure you are comparing quotes on equal terms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Does Leveling Cost for Common Jobs?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most leveling jobs are small enough that the per-square-foot rate is less useful than the total. Contractors price by the setup, the number of holes drilled, and the volume of material pumped, so a single slab and three adjacent slabs may differ in price by less than you expect. The table below shows typical US ranges for common residential jobs. Treat them as a starting point for a sanity check, not a quote: regional labor rates, access and void depth will move the real number.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. Area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mudjacking (typical)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Foam (typical)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
