                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-40 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$400-800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-1,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage apron</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-100 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$500-1,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$800-1,800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patio section</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-1,300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,000-2,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Several driveway panels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$900-2,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500-3,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pool deck or large driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400-800 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500-3,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,500-6,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice how much of the bottom of the table is set by minimums. Many leveling contractors will not roll a truck for less than a few hundred dollars, because drilling, pumping and patching take roughly the same setup whether they lift one square or four. If you have one sunken sidewalk square and two others that are starting to drop, it is usually cheaper per square foot to do all three in one visit than to call back next year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foam ranges are wider than mudjacking ranges because foam is priced partly by the pound. A contractor may quote a base rate plus a per-pound charge, or estimate the total pounds from the void size. A slab that has dropped half an inch over compacted soil may need only a modest amount of foam. A slab that has dropped two inches over soil washed out by a gutter downspout may need several times as much, and the bill rises accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Steps and stoops deserve a note. Concrete steps are heavy, often hollow underneath or poured over loose fill, and frequently pull away from the house. Lifting them is doable but harder to price before the contractor drills, because nobody knows how big the void is. Expect a wider quote range, and ask how the contractor will handle the joint against the foundation once the steps come back up, since that gap is where water got in to begin with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Makes Concrete Leveling More Expensive?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Void size is the biggest variable. Leveling works by filling the space under a slab with material until the slab lifts. A slab that settled because the fill beneath it compacted a little has a small, even void. A slab undermined by erosion from a downspout, a leaking pipe or a burrowing animal can sit over a cavity that takes far more material to fill. You cannot see the void from the surface, which is why honest contractors give a range or a per-pound rate for foam rather than a firm number before drilling.
          </p>

          <p>
            How far the slab has to rise also matters. Lifting a slab half an inch to clear a trip hazard is a short job. Bringing a garage apron up three inches so it meets the garage floor again takes more material, more time and more care to avoid cracking the slab as it moves. Big lifts also raise the odds that the slab will crack during the process, which some contractors note in the contract as a risk the homeowner accepts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Access and slab type add cost. A driveway the truck can park beside is easy. A back patio behind a fence, with hoses running through the yard, takes longer. Thicker slabs, slabs with heavy reinforcement and slabs tied to other structures such as steps or a foundation can be harder to lift evenly. Stamped, colored or exposed aggregate concrete costs more to patch afterward because the drill holes are harder to hide, and some contractors charge for color-matched patching.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hidden costs worth asking about</h3>

          <p>
            Several items are often left off a headline quote. Recaulking or sealing the joints after lifting may be extra. So may cutting a control joint, grinding a lip where two slabs still do not meet perfectly, or rerouting a downspout that caused the washout. If you live where permits are required for work in the public sidewalk, the city may require a permit or specific repair method. Ask each contractor to list exactly what is included, and price the extras separately so you know the real total.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, region and season move the number. Leveling contractors in high-cost metro areas often charge more per job than those in smaller markets, and spring is busy in cold climates because frost heave and snowmelt reveal settlement. If your job is not a safety emergency, asking for a quote in late summer or fall can sometimes shorten the wait, though it rarely changes the price dramatically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="methods" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Foam Worth Paying More Than Mudjacking?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mudjacking pumps a cement-based slurry, often a mix of soil or sand, cement and water, through holes typically around 1.5 to 2 inches across. Foam leveling injects expanding polyurethane through much smaller holes, usually around 5/8 of an inch. Both lift the slab by filling the void under pressure. The differences that matter to cost are weight, cure time, how long the repair lasts and how visible the holes are afterward. Our full <a href="/mudjacking-vs-polyjacking/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">mudjacking vs polyjacking comparison</a> covers each of those factors in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is the factor people underestimate. Slurry is heavy, on the order of 100 pounds or more per cubic foot, while lifting foam is light, typically just a few pounds per cubic foot. Adding a lot of heavy slurry on top of soft or poorly compacted soil can contribute to the slab settling again. Foam adds very little load. On weak soils or deep voids, that difference is a real argument for foam despite the higher price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cure time is the other practical difference. Foam usually reaches working strength quickly, and many contractors let you drive on a foam-lifted slab within roughly 15 to 30 minutes. Mudjacking slurry needs time to set, and contractors commonly ask you to keep vehicles off for at least a day, sometimes longer in cool weather. For a driveway you use every day, that can matter. For a back patio, it usually does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Longevity is where foam often justifies its price. Polyurethane does not absorb water and resists washing out, while slurry can erode over time if water keeps running under the slab. Mudjacking commonly lasts several years to a decade or more when the drainage problem is fixed, and foam is often expected to last longer, but neither method survives the original cause being ignored. If water caused the settling, either repair can fail again until the downspout, grading or leak is fixed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: mudjacking is a reasonable buy for small, budget jobs over stable soil, where waiting a day to drive on the slab is fine. Foam is worth the premium for driveways in daily use, slabs over soft or eroded soil, and decorative concrete where small holes matter. If two quotes come in close together, foam is usually the better value.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Is Replacing the Slab Cheaper Than Leveling It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leveling makes sense when the slab is structurally sound: one or two pieces, no major crumbling, and settlement that has left it tipped or dropped rather than shattered. It stops making sense when the concrete itself is failing. A slab broken into many pieces will lift unevenly, and each piece may need its own holes and attention. Heavily spalled, scaled or pitted concrete will still look and wear badly after it is lifted, so you may pay for leveling and then pay again for resurfacing or replacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age is the second test. Exterior concrete commonly lasts 25 to 30 years or more with reasonable care. If your driveway is near the end of that run, with widespread cracking and surface wear, spending $2,000 to lift a few panels may only buy a few years. In that case, it can be better value to put the money toward replacement, especially if you were planning to redo the driveway anyway. Our guide to <a href="/concrete-driveway-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete driveway cost</a> shows what a full repour typically runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ongoing movement is the third test. If the soil keeps shifting because of expansive clay, a high water table or a slope that is creeping, lifting the slab treats the symptom. Contractors can sometimes stabilize the soil with deeper injections, but if the slab has been lifted before and dropped again, ask what will be different this time. A soil or drainage fix may need to come first, and that changes the math.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Lift sound slabs, replace failing ones</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the concrete is in one or two solid pieces and the cause of settling can be fixed, level it. If it is shattered, crumbling or near the end of its life, put the money toward replacement instead of lifting it twice.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is a middle path for surfaces that are sound but ugly. A slab that has been lifted back to level but has a worn or stained top can sometimes be refreshed with a resurfacer rather than replaced. Products like the ones in our <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete resurfacer</a> roundup work only on structurally sound concrete, but they can stretch the life of a slab that is otherwise worth keeping, and they cost far less than a repour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Should You Seal Joints and Cracks After Leveling?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and it is the cheapest part of the job that most homeowners skip. Most settlement starts with water: rain running off a roof, along a foundation or through open joints washes fine soil out from under the slab, leaving a void. Once the slab is lifted, the same joints and cracks will keep letting water in unless you close them. Sealing them is the step that makes the leveling last.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the joints between slabs and between the slab and the house. These are usually filled with a flexible self-leveling polyurethane or silicone joint sealant, often over a foam backer rod in wider joints. Many leveling contractors offer this as an add-on, frequently priced per linear foot. If you plan to do it yourself, wait until the contractor's patch material in the drill holes has set, clean the joints, and follow the sealant's minimum temperature and joint-width guidance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, fill cracks. Lifting can open existing hairline cracks slightly, and sometimes creates a new one. Narrow cracks take a flexible crack sealant; wider ones may need backer rod first or a repair mortar for non-moving cracks. Our tested picks for the <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete crack filler</a> are sorted by crack width and whether the crack still moves, which is exactly the question to ask after a lift. Our <a href="/driveway-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">driveway crack repair</a> guide walks through the process step by step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then fix the drainage that caused the problem. Extend downspouts several feet away from slabs and foundations, regrade soil that slopes toward the concrete, and repair any leaking irrigation line or hose bib. Doing these small jobs costs little and is the single best predictor of whether you will pay for leveling again. Contractors see the same slabs come back when nobody moved the downspout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider sealing the slab surface itself once the joints are done. A penetrating sealer helps reduce water and deicing salt absorption, which slows freeze-thaw damage. It is not required for the lift to hold, but if you are already out there with caulk and a broom, it is a reasonable, inexpensive way to protect concrete you just paid to save.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quotes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Get a Fair Concrete Leveling Quote?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Get at least two or three quotes, and make sure each one covers the same scope. Write down the slabs you want lifted, the approximate drop at each, and any extras you want included such as joint sealing or crack filling. Ask each contractor to state the method, the number and size of holes, how they will patch the holes, any job minimum, and for foam, whether the price is fixed or based on pounds used with a cap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask about warranty in plain terms. Some contractors warranty the lift for a set number of years if the slab settles again, often with conditions about drainage. Read those conditions. A warranty that is voided if a downspout discharges near the slab is fair, but you should know about it before signing. A contractor who will not put any warranty in writing is a reason to keep looking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for red flags. A firm quote for foam without drilling may be fine if it includes a generous estimate, but a price that seems too low may hide a per-pound charge that balloons once work starts. Pressure to sign the same day, no proof of insurance, and no clear plan for the cause of settling are all warning signs. Leveling is a specialized trade, and a general handyman with a rented pump is not the same as a company that lifts slabs every day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, compare the leveling quote with at least a rough replacement price if the slab is borderline. If leveling comes in at 60 to 70 percent of replacement, and the concrete is older or cracked, replacement may be the better long-term spend. If leveling is a third of the replacement price and the slab is sound, it is usually an easy decision. Either way, knowing both numbers protects you from being sold the wrong job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the downspout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most sunken slabs were undermined by water. If you lift the concrete and leave the downspout dumping next to it, the soil keeps washing out and the slab drops again. Extend downspouts, fix grading and repair leaks before or right after leveling, or you are paying for the same repair twice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lifting a slab that should be replaced</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Leveling restores height, not condition. A driveway that is shattered, crumbling or near the end of its life will still be shattered and crumbling after it is lifted. Get a replacement price too, and spend on leveling only when the concrete itself is sound and worth keeping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing quotes with different scopes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One contractor may include hole patching, joint sealing and a warranty while another quotes only the lift. On paper the cheaper quote wins, but adding the extras later can erase the difference. List the slabs, the method, the extras and the warranty terms and make every bidder price the same job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on mudjacked concrete too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Slurry needs time to set. Parking on a freshly mudjacked driveway before the contractor says it is ready can push the slab back down or crack it. Foam lets you drive on the slab much sooner, but with mudjacking, follow the stated wait, which is commonly at least a day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving joints open after the lift</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The joints between slabs are where water gets under the concrete. Leaving them open after leveling invites the same erosion that caused the problem. Fill them with a flexible joint sealant, using backer rod in wide gaps, and fill cracks with a suitable crack filler once the drill-hole patches have set.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to level one sidewalk square?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually the contractor's minimum, which commonly falls around $300 to $800 depending on the company and region. The actual material for one square is small, but drilling, pumping and patching take setup time whatever the size. If neighboring squares are starting to drop, adding them to the same visit often costs only a little more and lowers the price per square foot considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is mudjacking cheaper than polyurethane foam?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Mudjacking commonly costs around $3 to $6 per square foot versus roughly $5 to $25 for foam. But foam uses smaller holes, adds far less weight to the soil, lets you drive on the slab in roughly 15 to 30 minutes, and resists washout. On soft soil or a daily-use driveway, the extra cost often pays for itself in longevity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete leveling last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends mostly on whether the cause of settling was fixed. With drainage corrected, mudjacking commonly lasts several years to a decade or more, and foam is often expected to last longer because it does not absorb water or erode. If water keeps running under the slab, either method can fail within a few seasons, which is why drainage fixes matter so much.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I level concrete myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DIY slab lifting is not practical for most homeowners. It needs specialized pumps, careful drilling and experience judging how fast to lift without cracking the slab. Small DIY foam kits exist, but they are aimed at filling voids, not controlled lifting. What you can do yourself is the follow-up: sealing joints, filling cracks and fixing downspouts and grading.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does homeowners insurance cover concrete leveling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most homeowners policies exclude settling, soil movement and wear, which covers the typical sunken driveway or sidewalk. There can be exceptions if a covered event, such as a sudden pipe burst, caused the washout, but that depends on your policy wording. Check with your insurer before assuming coverage, and document the damage with photos if you file a claim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will leveling fix cracks in my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Leveling raises the slab and can close a gap where one side dropped, but it does not repair cracks. Some cracks may tighten slightly when the slab is lifted, and lifting can occasionally create a new crack. Plan to fill cracks with a flexible crack sealant after the lift, and seal the joints so water stops getting under the concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can I use my driveway after leveling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With polyurethane foam, many contractors let you drive on the slab within roughly 15 to 30 minutes, because the foam reaches working strength quickly. With mudjacking, the slurry needs time to set, so contractors commonly ask you to keep vehicles off for at least 24 hours, sometimes longer in cool weather. Always follow the specific contractor's instructions.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

