        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most homeowners pay roughly $500 to $2,500 to level sunken concrete, with mudjacking at the low end per square foot and polyurethane foam at the high end. Either is usually far cheaper than replacement, as long as the slab is sound, in one or two pieces and not near the end of its life.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever method you choose, fix the water that caused the settling and seal the joints and cracks afterward. Those inexpensive steps decide whether you pay for leveling once or every few years. If the concrete is broken up or worn out, get a replacement price before you spend money lifting it.
          </p>
